    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Boat coffins and mourning taboos in the Visayas",
    text: "Visayans of the 16th century kept a dead person of rank in the house for days while women sang dirges, then buried the body in a hardwood coffin shaped like a boat, sometimes with a slave to serve them, gold and fine porcelain. Burial could be under the house, in caves, or on a hillside facing the sea. The family observed maglahi, a taboo on rice, and wore white or plain cloth; a widow cut her hair and stayed indoors. A chief's death might require a raid to take lives before mourning could end. Earlier, secondary burial of bones in jars was widespread.",
