    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Building the Ifugao terraces",
    text: "In the Cordillera of northern Luzon, the Ifugao built terraces up whole mountainsides, retaining walls of stone or packed mud holding pond fields fed from the forest above by bamboo pipes and channels. Recent excavation at Kiangan suggests the big terrace systems expanded around the 17th century, perhaps as people moved uphill away from Spanish power, though some began earlier. Fields were owned by families and inherited by the eldest child; the terraces passed down with the heirloom rice granary and its carved bulul guardian figures. Planting and harvest were led by the wealthiest family's field, and each step had its rite and priest.",
