    evidence: "documented",
    title: "The nine nights and the year's end of mourning",
    text: "Catholic Filipinos kept a wake, lamay, for several nights with the body at home while neighbours came to pray, eat and gamble to keep the family awake. After burial the family prayed a novena, pasiyam, for nine nights, ending with a feast. Mourners wore black and avoided parties, and on the first anniversary, babang luksa, the mourning clothes were put off with another meal. On the days of All Saints and All Souls, families went to the cemetery to clean the graves, light candles and spend the night eating beside their dead, a custom that blends old ancestral feasting with the Church calendar.",
