    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Irrigated taro and the sand drawings of Vanuatu",
    text: "On the high islands of Vanuatu, taro was grown in pond fields fed by stone-lined channels from streams, while yams, the prestige crop, filled dry gardens where their long tubers were measured and exhibited at harvest festivals. Villages drew water from springs and streams in bamboo tubes and coconut shells. Knowledge of gardens, genealogies and routes was held partly in sand drawings, sandroing, single continuous lines traced in the sand with one finger to tell a story or leave a message; on some islands they marked the path the dead must follow. UNESCO lists the drawings as intangible heritage.",
    wiki: ["Taro"],
