    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Kanakas in the Queensland cane fields",
    text: "Between 1863 and 1904 some 62,000 labour contracts brought islanders, mostly from Vanuatu and the Solomon Islands, to cut sugar cane in Queensland. Recruiters' ships worked island to island; many recruits signed willingly for three years to earn trade boxes of tools, cloth and guns, but kidnapping and deception, called blackbirding, were common in the early decades. Death rates on the plantations were several times those of Europeans. Under the White Australia policy most were deported between 1906 and 1908; those who stayed founded the Australian South Sea Islander community.",
    wiki: ["Blackbirding", "Australian South Sea Islanders"],
