    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Whale teeth, yaqona and mats in Fijian ceremonies",
    text: "No serious request in Fiji was made without a tabua, a polished sperm whale tooth on a plaited cord, presented with a speech. Tabua were given to ask for a bride, to seal an alliance, to beg forgiveness and at the reguregu, the ceremony of mourning, when kin brought whale teeth, mats, bark cloth and food to the house of the dead and kissed the body. Every such presentation was accompanied by drinking yaqona, kava, in strict order of rank. After a death the family kept a period of mourning, ending on the hundredth night with a feast. Tabua pass down lineages and are never sold.",
