    title: "Beating masi in Fiji",
    text: "Masi, Fijian bark cloth, was made by women from the inner bark of the paper mulberry, stripped from young saplings, soaked, and beaten on a wooden anvil with grooved clubs until strips spread and felted together. Sheets were joined with arrowroot paste and decorated with stencils cut from banana leaf or, in Lau and Moce, printed with rubbing boards, in rust red and black from soot and clay. Masi wrapped newborns and the dead, dressed chiefs at installation, and was piled in gift exchanges. Island communities specialised: Moce and Vatulele became known for their cloth, which they traded for food and canoes.",
