    title: "Girmit and the sugar lines of Fiji",
    text: "To spare Fijian villages from plantation labour, Governor Arthur Gordon brought in indentured workers from India from 1879. Over some 37 years about 60,000 came under a five-year contract, the girmit, mostly to cut cane for the Colonial Sugar Refining Company. They lived in barrack lines, suffered heavy tasks, fines and violence, and suicide rates were high. Fijians meanwhile were kept in villages under chiefly rule, their land mostly unalienable. After indenture ended in 1920 many Indo-Fijians stayed on leased land as smallholding cane farmers, creating the divided economy of colonial Fiji.",
