    text: "Around 1350 BCE people making finely stamped pottery appeared in the Bismarck Archipelago, and within about three centuries their descendants had sailed to Vanuatu, New Caledonia, Fiji, Tonga and Samoa, islands no one had lived on. They carried pigs, dogs, chickens, taro, yams, breadfruit and bananas in their outrigger canoes. They lived in stilt houses on beaches and reef flats, fished and gathered shellfish intensively, and ate out seabirds and giant tortoises on first arrival. Obsidian from New Britain travelled with them thousands of kilometres. They were the ancestors of the Polynesians and many other islanders.",
