    text: "Tongan farmers grew yams in large, well-kept gardens, with taro, plantains and breadfruit; the first yams of the season were presented to the Tuʻi Tonga at the ʻinasi ceremony. Food for feasts was cooked in the ʻumu, an earth oven of hot stones covered with leaves and earth, usually by men, while daily cooking was lighter. Breadfruit was fermented in pits lined with leaves to make a sour paste that kept for months, insurance against cyclones and drought. Pigs were raised for feasts and exchange rather than daily meals, and the order of serving at a feast marked everyone's rank.",
