    title: "Ahupuaʻa: from the mountain to the sea",
    text: "Hawaiian land was divided into ahupuaʻa, wedge-shaped districts usually running from the mountain ridge to the reef, so that each held forest, farmland and fisheries. A konohiki managed each for its chief, directing water in the irrigation ditches, ʻauwai, that fed the loʻi, flooded taro terraces, and imposing kapu seasons on fish and plants. Commoners, makaʻāinana, farmed the land and owed labour and produce, but were free to move to another chief's land if badly treated. On the dry leeward slopes, vast dryland field systems of sweet potato and dryland taro fed the chiefly courts of Kona and Kohala.",
