    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Walls in the sea: Hawaiian fishponds",
    text: "Hawaiians built fishponds, loko iʻa, by walling off shallow bays with rock walls up to hundreds of metres long. Sluice gates, mākāhā, of wooden grates let small fry swim in with the tide and kept fattened fish from leaving, so mullet and milkfish grew on algae in the brackish water. Some of the walls are believed to be more than 600 years old, built by chiefs with large levies of labour. The ponds were the property of chiefs and kept their tables supplied; commoners fished the open reef with nets, lines and traps, and followed kapu seasons that let spawning fish recover.",
