    text: "Until 1819, Hawaiian households lived under the ʻaikapu, the sacred eating rules. Men and women ate apart, in separate houses; men cooked the food for both; and women were forbidden pork, most bananas, coconut, certain fish and red foods tied to the gods. A household compound, kauhale, was a group of thatched houses: a men's eating house that also held the family shrine, a women's eating house, a sleeping house and work houses. Staples were poi, pounded taro, sweet potato and fish. In 1819, after Kamehameha I's death, his widow Kaʻahumanu and son Liholiho publicly ate together and ended the system.",
