    text: "Hawaiians held that the bones, iwi, carried a person's mana, and had to be protected from enemies who might use them. Commoners were usually buried in sand dunes, caves or near the house in a flexed position wrapped in kapa; for chiefs, the flesh was removed and the bones bundled and hidden by a trusted retainer in a secret cave, sometimes with the helper killed or sworn to silence so the place could not be known. Kin wailed, cut their hair, knocked out teeth or tattooed the tongue in grief. The dead might return as ʻaumakua, family guardians in animal form.",
