    text: "Māori ancestors brought the kūmara, sweet potato, from tropical Polynesia to a cold country and had to adapt it. Gardens were placed on warm north-facing slopes, their soil lightened with added gravel and sand, and planted in spring with rites to Rongo. Because kūmara rots in the cold, tubers were stored over winter in underground or semi-sunken pits, rua kūmara, dug into dry banks and roofed, whose remains dot the landscape. In the south, beyond where kūmara would grow, people lived on fern root, aruhe, which was dried, roasted and pounded, and on birds, fish and cabbage tree root.",
