    text: "The first settlers of Aotearoa, arriving around 1250 to 1300, found a land of flightless birds. Nine species of moa, some over two metres tall, were hunted with spears and snares and butchered at big camps like the Wairau Bar, where tools, eggshell and moa bone are piled deep. Within about two hundred years, moa and several other birds were extinct from hunting and burning of forest. Hunters turned to seals, smaller birds taken in snares and by spear, fish and gardening. Later Māori knew moa only by name, as a proverb for extinction: ka ngaro ā-moa, lost like the moa.",
