    text: "Pounamu, New Zealand greenstone, was found as boulders in the rivers of Te Tai Poutini on the South Island's west coast. Ngāi Tahu controlled the source from the 18th century and travelled over the Southern Alps to fetch it. It was so hard that shaping it meant weeks or months of sawing with sandstone and grit, and drilling with a pump drill. It became adzes, chisels, mere clubs and ornaments like the hei-tiki, which passed from one generation to the next, taking on the mana of their owners. Pounamu was carried north and traded for kūmara, fine cloaks and dried food.",
