    text: "With no paper mulberry thriving in the cold, Māori women turned to harakeke, New Zealand flax. They scraped its leaves with a mussel shell to free the silky muka fibre, rolled it on the thigh into thread and wove cloaks by finger-twining, whatu, without a loom, hanging the warp from two upright pegs. The finest cloaks, kahu huruhuru and kahu kiwi, were covered in feathers. Harakeke also made baskets, kete, mats, nets and ropes. Weavers observed tapu while working and the art was passed from elder women to chosen girls. In the 1820s flax fibre became a main export for muskets.",
