    text: "When a Māori person dies the body is brought to the marae and lies in state, usually for three days, never left alone, while groups of mourners arrive in turn to be called on by women's karanga, to weep, and to speak directly to the dead in oratory before addressing the living. After burial the house of the dead is cleansed of tapu with prayers and water, and the family shares a meal that lifts the restrictions. In earlier times bodies were often exposed or buried, then the bones recovered, cleaned and hidden in caves in a second rite, hahunga. The unveiling of the headstone, a year later, is the later form.",
