    text: "Every Māori person belonged by whakapapa, genealogy, to a whānau, extended family, within a hapū, a sub-tribe that held land and fought as a unit, within an iwi. Descent could be traced through either parent, and a person had rights where they kept up their ties by living on and using the land, ahi kā, keeping the fires burning. Chiefly marriages were arranged to make peace or alliance between hapū, sometimes promised in childhood, taumau, and women of rank could refuse. Among commoners courtship was freer, and marriage was marked by gift exchanges between the families rather than a single rite.",
