    text: "In the 1840s and 1850s Māori communities were the main farmers of New Zealand: they grew wheat and potatoes, built flour mills and owned coastal schooners that supplied Auckland and sent grain to Australia. After the New Zealand Wars of the 1860s the government confiscated land in Waikato and Taranaki, and the Native Land Court turned collective title into individual shares that could be bought piecemeal. By 1900 Māori held about a sixth of the North Island. Communities like Parihaka resisted peacefully, and many families became seasonal labourers, shearers and gum-diggers on land that had been theirs.",
