    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Stone mulch and manavai on Rapa Nui",
    text: "On windswept, dry Rapa Nui the islanders farmed by covering fields with broken stone, lithic mulch, which held moisture, warmed the soil and slowed erosion; they turned great areas of the island into rock gardens for sweet potato, taro, yams and bananas. They built manavai, circular stone-walled enclosures, to shelter bananas and paper mulberry from the wind. With no permanent streams, water came from springs at the coast, where fresh water seeped out at low tide, and from the crater lakes of Rano Kau and Rano Raraku. Chickens, kept in stone henhouses, hare moa, were the main livestock.",
