    text: "In 1862 and 1863 Peruvian slavers took about 1,500 Rapa Nui people to work in Peru; most died, and a few survivors brought back smallpox. With missionary removals, the population fell to 111 by 1877. Chile annexed the island in 1888 and leased almost all of it to a company, later Williamson-Balfour, that ran it as a sheep ranch. From the 1910s the Rapa Nui were confined to the village of Hanga Roa behind a wall and needed permission to leave it, while sheep grazed the rest. They worked for the company as shepherds and shearers. Only in 1966 did they gain Chilean citizenship.",
