    text: "In the Society Islands each family, lineage and chiefdom had its marae, a paved stone court with a raised platform, ahu, where priests offered pigs, fish, bark cloth and at times human sacrifices to the gods, above all ʻOro, the god of war. The ʻarioi were a society of performers dedicated to ʻOro who travelled between islands in fleets of canoes, staging dances, comic plays and feasts at harvest time, graded by tattoo. Members were forbidden to raise children and killed newborns unless they left the society. Missionaries of the London Missionary Society, arriving in 1797, won Pōmare II in 1812 and ended the ʻarioi.",
