    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Breadfruit seasons and the mahi pit",
    text: "Breadfruit trees supplied Tahiti with food most of the year, and explorers from Cook's time were amazed at how little labour it took; Bligh's voyage on the Bounty was sent in 1787 to carry breadfruit saplings to feed slaves in the Caribbean. Fruit were baked whole in the earth oven, ahimaʻa, or pounded into poi with banana and taro. For the months when the trees rested, surplus fruit was peeled and packed into leaf-lined pits to ferment into mahi, a sour paste that kept for years. Coconuts, fish, shellfish, pigs and dogs rounded out the diet, and men and women ate apart.",
