    title: "Canoes and star paths of the Caroline Islands",
    text: "Carolinian navigators sailed their outrigger canoes across hundreds of kilometres of open sea without instruments, holding in memory a star compass of the rising and setting points of some thirty stars, the swell patterns, and the etak system, in which a reference island imagined moving under the stars marked the canoe's progress. Knowledge was taught by elder navigators to chosen young men, in canoe houses and by pebbles laid out in a circle, and guarded by schools such as Warieng and Fanur. Canoes were built of breadfruit wood, sewn with coconut sennit and caulked with breadfruit sap. Mau Piailug of Satawal later helped revive Polynesian voyaging.",
