    text: "From about 900 CE, Chamorro houses in the Mariana Islands stood on pairs of stone pillars, latte, each a limestone or basalt shaft, haligi, topped by a hemispherical capstone, tasa, that kept rats out and lifted the building above floods. Latte houses came in rows, the largest perhaps for canoes or men's gatherings. The dead were often buried among the pillars, so that the ancestors lay under the household. Chamorro society was matrilineal, with clans ranked noble and commoner, and ancestors' skulls were kept in the house. Spanish conquest after 1668 and resettlement ended latte building.",
