    text: "Japan took Germany's Micronesian islands in 1914 and ran them under a League of Nations mandate from 1920. Its Nan'yō Kōhatsu company turned Saipan, Tinian and Rota into sugar cane plantations worked mostly by settlers from Okinawa; by the late 1930s Japanese outnumbered islanders. Chamorro and Carolinian people were pushed into lower-paid work and schooled for three years in Japanese. Phosphate was mined on Angaur and copra bought from atoll villages. In the Second World War the islands became fortresses and battlefields, and islanders were forced into labour, displaced and killed.",
