    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Cutting copra for the trader's store",
    text: "From the 1860s the main cash crop across the western Pacific was copra, dried coconut meat, pressed abroad for oil used in soap and margarine. Village families split mature nuts, scooped out the meat, and dried it in the sun or over fires in smoke houses, then sold it by the bag to a trader's store or a visiting schooner for tobacco, cloth, tinned fish, kerosene and cash to pay colonial head taxes. German companies such as Godeffroy in Samoa and New Guinea, and later Lever's plantations in the Solomons, grew coconuts on a large scale with contract labour. Prices rose and crashed with world markets.",
